A||||16||Ferrari_cdn||Advantages/Disadvantages of 3800 and 4.9||||1||
Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||11-06-2001||01:37 PM||||I know there has been quite some discussion between the 4.9L and the 3800SC, but WHAT are the advantages of using the 3800SC OR the 4.9L Caddy V8, and the disadvantages of using either, and why did you go for that motor or the other?<P>Isn't the 3800SC a V6?  Why go for a V6 over a V8?  I'm not an expert in motors so I just want to learn more clearly about them.<P>I am planning on a Caddy 4.9L V8 but I saw a few people rather going for the 3800.  What makes it so better?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">  </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ferrari_cdn||11-06-2001||08:07 PM||||*bump*||||reg||12
Z||000002||samoht||11-06-2001||08:19 PM||||I couldnt afford the 3800 or 4.9 So I went with the 3.2, but if I had the cash I would have gone with the Super Charged 3800 .. Dependable power ..  4.9 is good too.. cheaper..  but stock to stock .. Definately the 3800<P>85gt3.2||||reg||1
Z||000003||Daleford||11-06-2001||09:11 PM||||See page 3.... <BR>3800 vs. 4.9 Caddy<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000004||jeffndebrus||11-06-2001||10:47 PM||||In my research I have found that there is no one perfect engine for the firo swap <P>Everyone has an oppinion that is what makes each one of us different and special to our moms.<P>This said, it appears that the main reason people are going for the 4.9 is that they are plentiful, cheap, not hard to make work in the fiero, and some people just like the idea of having a v8 four inches behind their right elbow.<P>If you are looking for very similar performance with the added benefit of incredible reliability-- then the 3800 would be a better choice.  (also the 3800 gets 36mpg in the fiero)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||11-06-2001||11:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jeffndebrus:<BR><B>In my research I have found that there is no one perfect engine for the firo swap <P>Everyone has an oppinion that is what makes each one of us different and special to our moms.<P>This said, it appears that the main reason people are going for the 4.9 is that they are plentiful, cheap, not hard to make work in the fiero, and some people just like the idea of having a v8 four inches behind their right elbow.<P>If you are looking for very similar performance with the added benefit of incredible reliability-- then the 3800 would be a better choice.  (also the 3800 gets 36mpg in the fiero)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't know about the reliability thing... That alum v8 lasts FOREVER...  The major PITA is that it can be tricky to rebuild  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> In terms of raw power, the 4.9 tops out a little more, and with a little creativity, (concerning the achilles heel top-end) has MUCH more potential...<P>In other words, the Caddy is an easier swap, but harder to find parts for and rebuild, but is cheap and plentiful in scrapyard form.<P>The 3800 has more of an aftermarket following (i.e., you can put a non-custom ground cam in it *gasp*) and is almost as plentiful. You don't need to monkey around with different model-years, but the physical swap is more demanding, or so my research tells me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000006||DrDave||11-06-2001||11:14 PM||||     The reason I picked the 4.9 is weight.  It has more than enough low end torque.  I'm hoping that we can move some of that low-end torque into horsepower.  I'd really like to see some power up around 5500rpm.  <BR>   But as for weight, It seems as tho I can get the rear-end to come around WAY too easy in stock form.  I don't need ANY more weight in the rear.||||reg||1
Z||000007||jeffndebrus||11-07-2001||01:28 PM||||I am strongly cosidering a 4.0 (aurora) northstar swap. Lots more valves! mmmmmmmm<P>Just have some more research to do.<P>Any comments or info would be welcome.||||reg||1
Z||000008||jeffndebrus||11-07-2001||05:14 PM||||bumpity bump bump bumpity bump||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||11-08-2001||11:51 PM||||Don't these motors red-out (and hit an ECU rev limiter hard) at a measely 4500rpm?!<P>Kick my booty if I'm spouting bs here.<P>But that's just too damned low for any fun.<P>Hopefully someone will figure out a way to throw a roller valvetrain or such onto this motor and at least get it up to ~5500 or so.<P>What about the 3800SC, will it wind out any harder than the stock 2.8 even?  (never driven one.)<P>Makes me glad I'm going with a Northstar, 6750 red line is good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  (I like revy engines that make power everywhere.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>Aurora should be similar.(It's a slightly narrower-bore Northstar)<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DrDave:<BR><B>     The reason I picked the 4.9 is weight.  It has more than enough low end torque.  I'm hoping that we can move some of that low-end torque into horsepower.  I'd really like to see some power up around 5500rpm.  <BR>   But as for weight, It seems as tho I can get the rear-end to come around WAY too easy in stock form.  I don't need ANY more weight in the rear.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||11-09-2001||12:24 AM||||It's a 4800RPM-limited motor. Concievably, it'll hold together to 5200 RPM...<BR>Yes, that's pretty sad.<P>On the other hand, it IS a torque-beast. If it's going into my car, I'm paying for custom rockers, and some stiffer springs to solve the mushy-topend problem  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>The other thing to consider is that the 3800 is a BOAT ANCHOR of a motor. Meanwhile, the 4.5/4.9s weigh in 10lbs heavier than the 2.8L... That's good for something. <P>The final thing to remember is that there *is* an aftermarket for the 3800. There's nothing for the 4.5/4.9. I'd have to get some custom springs/rockers to make this work the way I want. Good thing the chevy 350TPI bolts up, huh? At least the intake/fuel delivery is taken care of  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000011||ray b||11-09-2001||01:44 AM||||what 350 TPI bolts to what????first i have heard that one about a caddy 4.9 who said that????<BR>i have heard a idea about making a cpu from a 350 chevy run a northstar or trying to anyway.<BR>will anyother cars valve springs fit the 4.9 like the olds rockers do?? can springs be doubled or does it share a common sized hyd-lifter,or can be bored out  lifters bores to use a diffrent cars hi-speed lifters????<BR>under grind the camshaft for more lift??? will that work??? [remove metal from bottom of cam away from lobes to fake more lift]<BR>how fast will bottom end spin and live???<BR>will other cars rods fit or be made to with some work???<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000012||Wannabe||11-09-2001||09:28 AM||||Pleeeeez don't say the 3800 is a boat anchor! Granted, it isn't the most compact, light weight package out there, but considering it's history and the fact that it is all iron, it's not bad. My first engine choice was N*, then SBC. When I realized neither one would happen for me before next racing season, I looked at the 4.9, but there are still too many question marks with that engine, particulary for the type of use I have in mind for it. The 3.8SC will add about  60 pounds over the 2.8. Not nearly enough to kill the handling. There are some simple mods that will let the engine pull freely to 6000 rpm, somthing that is not now and may never be available for the Caddy 4.9 at a reasonable price. Not a big deal if the car will be just street driven or dragged, or even autocrossed, but a real downer if you play on road courses. Let's face it - there is no point in dumping on any of these engine alternatives. They all have their good points and not so good points. Which one you choose depends on personal preferences, needs and resources. The amazing thing is, as Fiero enthusiasts, look at how many excellent alternatives we have to choose from! ||||reg||1
Z||000013||rockcrawl||11-09-2001||04:21 PM||||A chevy TPI intake will not bolt to a Cadillac 4.9 engine, but the ECM can be used. The Olds rockers will fit, but will not provide any increase in valve lift. That was just bs brought about by someone who's name I won't mention. The Olds rockers <B>will</B> cause premature wear to the valves. The 4.9 already has roller lifters. We are working on a reground cam.||||reg||1
Z||000014||ray b||11-09-2001||05:21 PM||||Rockcrawl you know the most about caddys 4.9<BR>what top rpm are you getting<BR>is the rpm limit valves springs, lifter pumpup, or ecm limited [does chevy ecm raze rpm limit] or fear of bottom end ?????<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000015||rockcrawl||11-09-2001||05:50 PM||||The Caddy ECM limits the RPM. The TPI ECM I'm using can be programmed to whatever limit I want, I have it at 6000. The car I have now is a 4T60 automatic, it shifts at 4500 with the pedal floored. If I hold it in a lower gear it will rev beyond 4500, but doesn't accelerate any more and it sounds a little funny at that speed, so there's no reason for me keep revving it that high. The rotating assembly of these engines is pretty strong, I'm sure it will take 6000 without trouble. The top end is the limiting factor, not sure if it's cam profile or intake restriction or what. The springs are very weak compared to SBC springs, maybe a spring upgrade is all it would need to get a few more Rs. I'm no expert on engines, but we have a pretty good assembly of people working on figuring it out.||||reg||7
Z||000016||fieroX||11-09-2001||10:51 PM||||With a few mods the 3800 (which to some will cost some money those with resources it wont alot) can make 350 crankshaft horsepower, remain totally streetable, and make 25+ mpg.  the caddy has extremely minimal aftermartet which makes modding difficult.  boat anchor? hardly, Id put a L67 vs a caddy any day.  see ya later.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif"> ||||reg||1
